= Lexical semantics — Technical =

== Representation problem ==
Lexical theories differ over whether entries encode:

* necessary/sufficient features;
* prototypes or exemplars;
* frames;
* typed qualia structure;
* event structures;
* argument structure;
* conceptual schemas;
* distributional representations;
* underspecified semantic potentials.

These are not mutually interchangeable.

== Relations ==
If lexical meanings are represented as predicates or concepts, relations may include subsumption, incompatibility, antonymy, meronymy, synonymy, troponymy, and entailment.

Lexical resources such as WordNet operationalize selected relations but do not exhaust lexical semantics.

== Sense granularity ==
A central problem is deciding when contextual variation constitutes a distinct sense.

Fine-grained inventories improve descriptive specificity but can reduce annotation agreement and computational robustness.

Coarse inventories risk collapsing semantically important distinctions.

== Lexicon–grammar interface ==
Argument structure, alternations, coercion, idioms, light verbs, aspectual class, and constructional meaning challenge a strict division between lexical entries and grammar.

== Distributional interface ==
Distributional models infer semantic structure from usage patterns. They can predict similarity and contextual behavior but require interpretation before geometric relations can be identified with lexical-semantic relations.

